    <div
      class="tab-content p-2 sm:p-6 max-w-full overflow-x-auto hidden peer-checked/tab1:block"
    >
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl w-full">
        <!-- All your CRUD content here, e.g.: -->
        <h1 class="text-center text-3xl font-bold border-b-4">
          Normal Crud
        </h1>

        <!-- CONNECTION Section -->
        <div class="mt-5">
          <h1 class="font-bold text-2xl">CONNECTION</h1>
          <p>
            To connect to a MySQL database called <code>dbhospital</code> hosted
            on
            <code>localhost</code> using the username <code>root</code> and no password.
            If the connection fails, it displays an error message.
          </p>

          <div
            class="bg-[#23272e] text-[#f8f8f2] p-4 rounded-md mt-4 overflow-x-auto max-w-full"
          >
            <pre
              class="whitespace-pre text-sm">
               <code class="language-php">
&lt;?php
    $servername = "localhost";
    $dbusername = "root";
    $dbpassword = "";
    $dbname = "database_name";

    try &#123;
        $conn = new PDO("mysql:host=$servername;dbname=$dbname", $dbusername, $dbpassword);
        $conn->setAttribute(PDO::ATTR_ERRMODE, PDO::ERRMODE_EXCEPTION);

    &#125; catch(PDOException $e) &#123;
        echo "Connection failed: " . $e->getMessage();
        die;  
    &#125;
?&gt;
                </code>
            </pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- PHP Backend Code Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h1 class="text-center font-bold text-2xl">PHP Backend Code</h1>
          <p>
            The purpose of your PHP code is to act as a simple back-end API that
            allows you to create, read, update, and delete (CRUD) user records
            in a database via HTTP requests, using Object-Oriented Programming
            (OOP) for better structure and reusability.
          </p>
          <h1 class="font-bold text-2xl border-b-4 w-fit pr-3 mt-3">Class</h1>
          <p>
            a class is a blueprint for creating objects. It helps organize code
            and group related functionality together. The main purpose of using
            a class is to implement Object-Oriented Programming (OOP), which
            improves modularity, reusability, and maintainability.
          </p>
          <div
            class="bg-[#23272e] text-[#f8f8f2] p-4 rounded-md mt-4 overflow-x-auto max-w-full"
          >
            <pre
              class="whitespace-pre text-sm">
               <code class="language-php">
&lt;?php
class User  &#123
    function save($json)&#123
      //put here the save function logic
    &#125;
    function getRecords()&#123
      //put here the getRecords function logic
    &#125;
    function update($json)&#123
      //put here the update function logic
    &#125;
    function delete($json)&#123
      //put here the delete function logic
    &#125;
 &#125
?&gt;
                </code>
            </pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Function save Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h1 class="font-bold text-2xl border-b-4 w-fit pr-3 mt-3">
            Function save
          </h1>
          <p>
            it can be use to save data to the database. It should be able to
            handle the data sent from the frontend and save it to database.
          </p>

          <div
            class="bg-[#23272e] text-[#f8f8f2] p-4 rounded-md mt-4 overflow-x-auto max-w-full"
          >
            <pre
              class="whitespace-pre text-sm">
               <code class="language-php">
&lt;?php

    function save($json)&#123
    include "connection.php";

    // Decode the JSON string into a PHP associative array
    $json = json_decode($json, true);

    // Extract individual values from the JSON array
    $fname = $json['fname'];   // First name from the input
    $mname = $json['mname'];   // Middle name from the input
    $lname = $json['lname'];   // Last name from the input

    // Prepare an SQL query to insert a new user record into the 'user' table
    $sql = "INSERT INTO user (fname, mname, lname) VALUES (:firstName, :middlename, :lastName)";

    // Use PDO's prepare method to safely prepare the SQL statement
    $stmt = $conn->prepare($sql);

    // Bind the actual values to the named placeholders in the SQL query
    $stmt->bindParam(":firstName", $fname);
    $stmt->bindParam(":middlename", $mname);
    $stmt->bindParam(":lastName", $lname);

    // Execute the prepared statement (insert the data into the database)
    $stmt->execute();

    // Check if the insert was successful (rowCount > 0 means success)
    $returnValue = $stmt->rowCount() > 0 ? 1 : 0;

    // Return 1 if successful, 0 if not
    return $returnValue;
    &#125;
    
?&gt;
                </code>
            </pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Function getRecords Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h1 class="font-bold text-2xl border-b-4 w-fit pr-3 mt-3">
            Function getRecords
          </h1>
          <p>
            The purpose of the getRecords() function is to retrieve (Read) all
            user records from the user table in your database and return them as
            a JSON-encoded array.
          </p>

          <div
            class="bg-[#23272e] text-[#f8f8f2] p-4 rounded-md mt-4 overflow-x-auto max-w-full"
          >
            <pre
              class="whitespace-pre text-sm">
               <code class="language-php">
&lt;?php

    function getRecords()&#123
    include "connection.php";

    // SQL query to get all users
    $sql = "SELECT * FROM user";  

    // Prepare the SQL statement       
    $stmt = $conn->prepare($sql);    

    // Execute the SQL query     
    $stmt->execute();            

    // Fetch all results as associative array          
    $returnValue = $stmt->fetchAll(PDO::FETCH_ASSOC);

    // Clean up (close connection) 
    $conn = null; $stmt = null;      

    // Return results in JSON format      
    return json_encode($returnValue);      
    &#125;
    
?&gt;
                </code>
            </pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Function update Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h1 class="font-bold text-2xl border-b-4 w-fit pr-3 mt-3">
            Function update
          </h1>
          <p>
            The purpose of the delete($json) function is to delete a user from
            the database based on their id.
          </p>

          <div
            class="bg-[#23272e] text-[#f8f8f2] p-4 rounded-md mt-4 overflow-x-auto max-w-full"
          >
            <pre
              class="whitespace-pre text-sm">
               <code class="language-php">
&lt;?php

    function getRecords()&#123
    include "connection.php";

    // Convert JSON string to PHP associative array
    $json = json_decode($json, true); 

    // Extract user data from JSON
    $id = $json['id'];
    $fname = $json['fname'];
    $mname = $json['mname'];
    $lname = $json['lname'];

    // SQL query to update user data where id matches
    $sql = "UPDATE user SET fname = :fname, mname = :mname, lname = :lname WHERE id = :id";
    
    // Prepare SQL statement
    $stmt = $conn->prepare($sql); 

    // Bind values to named parameters
    $stmt->bindParam(":fname", $fname);
    $stmt->bindParam(":mname", $mname);
    $stmt->bindParam(":lname", $lname);
    $stmt->bindParam(":id", $id);
    
    // Execute the update
    $stmt->execute(); 

    // If rows were updated, return 1; otherwise, return 0
    $returnValue = $stmt->rowCount() > 0 ? 1 : 0;
    return $returnValue;
    &#125
    
?&gt;
                </code>
            </pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Function delete Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h1 class="font-bold text-2xl border-b-4 w-fit pr-3 mt-3">
            Function delete
          </h1>
          <p>
            The purpose of the update($json) function is to update an existing
            user's data (first name, middle name, last name) in the user table
            based on their id.
          </p>

          <div
            class="bg-[#23272e] text-[#f8f8f2] p-4 rounded-md mt-4 overflow-x-auto max-w-full"
          >
            <pre
              class="whitespace-pre text-sm">
               <code class="language-php">
&lt;?php

    function getRecords()&#123
    include "connection.php";

    // Convert JSON string to PHP associative array
    $json = json_decode($json, true); 

    // Extract the user ID to delete
    $id = $json['id']; 

    // Prepare SQL query to delete the user
    $sql = "DELETE FROM user WHERE id = :id";
    $stmt = $conn->prepare($sql);

    // Bind the ID value
    $stmt->bindParam(":id", $id); 
    
    // Run the delete operation
    $stmt->execute(); 

    // Return 1 if a row was deleted, 0 if not
    $returnValue = $stmt->rowCount() > 0 ? 1 : 0;
    return $returnValue;
    &#125
    
?&gt;
                </code>
            </pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- API Endpoint Handler Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h1 class="font-bold text-2xl border-b-4 w-fit pr-3 mt-3">
            API Endpoint Handler
          </h1>
          <p>
            To route requests to the appropriate method of the User class based
            on what the client wants to do save, get, update, or delete a user.
          </p>

          <div
            class="bg-[#23272e] text-[#f8f8f2] p-4 rounded-md mt-4 overflow-x-auto max-w-full"
          >
            <pre
              class="whitespace-pre text-sm">
               <code class="language-php">
&lt;?php

// Get the 'json' data from the POST request. If not set, default to an empty string.
$json = isset($_POST['json']) ? $_POST['json'] : "";

// Get the 'operation' type from the POST request (e.g., "save", "getRecords", etc.)
$operation = $_POST['operation'];

// Create a new instance of the User class so we can call its methods
$user = new User();

// Use a switch statement to determine which operation to perform
switch($operation)&#123

    // If the operation is "save", call the save() method and echo (output) the result
    case "save":
        echo $user->save($json);
        break;

    // If the operation is "getRecords", call the getRecords() method and echo the result
    case "getRecords":
        echo $user->getRecords();
        break;

    // If the operation is "update", call the update() method with the JSON data and echo the result
    case "update":
        echo $user->update($json);
        break;

    // If the operation is "delete", call the delete() method with the JSON data and echo the result
    case "delete":
        echo $user->delete($json);
        break;
&#125

    
?&gt;
                </code>
            </pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- HTML Code Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h1 class="text-center font-bold text-2xl">HTML Code</h1>
          <p>
            HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is the standard language used to
            create and structure content on the web. Its main purpose is to
            define the structure and layout of a web page.
          </p>
          <h1 class="font-bold text-2xl border-b-4 w-fit pr-3 mt-3">
            Libraries and Frameworks
          </h1>
          <p>
            Libraries and frameworks are collections of pre-written code that
